WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.540
REDIKER: Good morning.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:11.110
I'm Doug Rediker, and I have been told that
I don't need to do the usual "turn off your

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.840
devices, et cetera" thing.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.780
So we're going to get right into it this morning,
if I could.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:22.132
The program is going to be that we're going
to speak with Gavin for about 10 minutes,

00:00:22.132 --> 00:00:24.360
and basically let Gavin explain.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.310
This is a complicated and brand-new subject
for a lot of us here.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:33.330
So we're going to ask Gavin to sort of go
through who he is, how he got here, and what

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.020
the heck Bitcoin is all about.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.990
And then I'm going to try and provoke some
interesting responses.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:45.270
And then hopefully, we'll have provoked enough
from the audience, that we'll get the rest

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:48.380
of the morning to have everybody ask their
questions.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:53.110
And Gavin will, I'm sure, answer every single
question, so that we'll have no questions

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:55.329
about Bitcoin when we leave.

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:57.170
Maybe we'll get there, maybe we won't.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:03.760
But let's start with Gavin Andresen, who is
the chief science officer.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:04.760
ANDRESEN: Chief scientist.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:11.970
REDIKER: Chief scientist -- no officers in
Bitcoin Foundation -- for the Bitcoin Foundation.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:17.490
And as I said, Gavin, if you can just sort
of go through who you are, how you got here,

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:23.340
and what in your view succinctly we need to
know about Bitcoin, before we go into some

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.870
of the "I don't understand it, so please explain,"
fill in the blank.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.010
ANDRESEN: Sure.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:30.310
I'm a technology guy.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.690
So I started my career working in Silicon
Valley.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:39.220
I worked for a company called "Silicon Graphics"
for eight years, creating 3D graphic software.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.939
After that, I actually did a series of startups,
some of which were successful, some of which

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:44.939
were miserable failures.

00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:49.510
So kind of a typical, you know, high-tech,
Silicon Valley background.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:58.299
When my kids were born in the year 2000, I
actually started working half-time, just spend

00:01:58.299 --> 00:02:03.619
more time with the kids, kind of step back
from the entrepreneurial, high-tech, start-up

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:05.210
life.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:12.730
And then in 2010, I stumbled across this interesting
little open-source software project called

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:13.730
"Bitcoin."

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:15.150
And I was kind of looking around for something
to do.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.879
You know, my kids were at an age where I didn't
-- I felt like I could jump into something

00:02:19.879 --> 00:02:24.060
more meaty.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:25.639
And Bitcoin caught my interest.

00:02:25.639 --> 00:02:31.540
Bitcoin combined kind of an interest I had
in economics, in computer cryptography, and

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:39.120
in distributed peer-to-peer systems, so systems
where there's no central control.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.129
I decided to do the simplest possible project
I could think of.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:47.239
I'm a technology guy, so obviously, you know,
if I see some new technology, I want to play

00:02:47.239 --> 00:02:48.239
with it.

00:02:48.239 --> 00:02:51.330
And I figured a lot of other people would
probably want to play with Bitcoin, too.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:56.390
But at that time, it wasn't that easy to get
Bitcoin.

00:02:56.390 --> 00:03:02.319
And so I decided that the easiest possible
project for me to tackle would be a website

00:03:02.319 --> 00:03:04.250
that just gave Bitcoin away.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:11.159
And so I spent $50 of my own money, and I
bought 10,000 bitcoins.

00:03:11.159 --> 00:03:16.799
At that time, bitcoins were selling for about
half-a-penny apiece.

00:03:16.799 --> 00:03:20.159
And I started giving bitcoins away at what's
called the "Bitcoin Faucet."

00:03:20.159 --> 00:03:28.209
And so turns out, if you give something away,
that's a good way to make people like you.

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:34.209
So a lot of people kind of liked that I was
giving these bitcoins away so people could

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:39.139
play with them, try them, you know, see that
the system worked, see that, you know, their

00:03:39.139 --> 00:03:41.219
transactions didn't get lost.

00:03:41.219 --> 00:03:46.980
And I slowly got more and more involved in
the project.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:53.010
I started contributing code to the project,
which at that time, was basically a one-person

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.010
project.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:59.799
I mean, the creator of Bitcoin's this person,
Satoshi Nakamoto, who I won't say upfront,

00:03:59.799 --> 00:04:01.409
I'm not Satoshi.

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:02.640
Don't know who Satoshi is.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.970
I haven't met him, or her, or them.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:12.809
Until at the beginning of 2011, Satoshi decided
to step back from the project, and kind of

00:04:12.809 --> 00:04:17.270
push me forward as the lead developer for
this open-source project that was still, back

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:21.570
then, just getting off the ground.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.950
Since then, my life has been kind of endlessly
fascinating.

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:28.790
So all sorts of interesting adventures through
Bitcoin.

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:34.800
And obviously, you know, Bitcoin has been
wildly successful, I hope, partly, because,

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.800
you know, I did give away 10,0000 bitcoins
way back then.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.050
I like -- at least, I like to tell myself
that that's part of the reason why Bitcoin

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.050
became successful.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:47.350
It was a way of bootstrapping.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:52.360
You know, it's a really interesting question,
right.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:55.160
I mean, bitcoins were worthless when they
were first created.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.730
They had no value, none at all.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:04.010
You know, when I bought those 10,000 bitcoins
to fund the Bitcoin Faucet, they were worth

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:05.160
under half-a-penny apiece.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:10.680
I didn't realize that at that time, but it
had just been a few weeks earlier that bitcoins

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.230
actually had a price, that there was actually
an exchange where people were starting to

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:16.880
trade bitcoins for dollars.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:24.280
And that's how we know the price of Bitcoin
is -- these exchanges run by whoever wants

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:29.370
to start an exchange, all over the world,
where people will trade bitcoins for dollars,

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:36.000
or euros, or yen, or whatever other thing
they want to trade bitcoins for.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.060
Today, bitcoins are trading for about $800
apiece.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:47.270
So those 10,000 bitcoins that I bought back
then, if I had kept them, would be worth about

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:52.080
$8 million today.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:06:02.000
The reason bitcoins are valuable, the reason
people want them, are because they're limited

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.000
in supply.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.950
There's a purely fixed number of bitcoins
that are being created.

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:12.300
Right now I believe there are about 12 million
bitcoins in existence.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.850
And the rules of the software, the open-source
software that I work on, there will never

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:19.050
be more than 21 million bitcoins in existence.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:25.800
So this is different than other kinds of money,
where -- you know, we don't know what the

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.200
Federal Reserve is going to do next year,
or 10 years from now.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:36.230
With Bitcoin, there's some certainty, because
it's written in the code, that there shall

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:38.540
only ever be 21 million bitcoins.

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:44.970
We can talk more about how hard and fast is
that rule, what other kinds of rules are there.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.020
But that's basically where Bitcoin gets its
value, right.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:51.780
We have these digital tokens that people have
decided have some value.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.730
We started with they're worth half-a-penny
apiece.

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.480
"I might as well buy some.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.460
Who knows if this project is going to be successful?

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:01.300
If it is, then I'll make a little money, maybe.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:05.340
If not, then maybe I'll have fun trading it
with my friends, maybe buying some alpaca

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.710
socks with my bitcoins."

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:12.260
It's actually alpaca socks were actually one
of the very first physical goods that you

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:13.260
could buy with bitcoins.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.930
And I have a couple pair of alpaca socks that
I bought for bitcoins.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:23.480
Because the alpaca socks guy actually lives
across the river from me up in Massachusetts.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:25.060
But that's the way a money works.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:29.300
I mean, we don't usually think about money
much.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:33.280
But all moneys are really kind of the shared
delusion that they're valuable because we

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:38.090
say it's valuable, and because there's a fixed
supply, because you can't just -- "Money doesn't

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.890
grow on trees, is the saying.

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:43.600
So that's what Bitcoin is.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:46.380
And that's what Bitcoin has been.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.040
What Bitcoin will become in the future, there's
lots and lots of speculation, because it turns

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:58.340
out, you can use Bitcoin for money, you can
use it as a payment network.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:03.530
It's incredibly convenient to use Bitcoin
to pay people face-to-face, using your cell

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:08.720
phones, or across the world, which is really
revolutionary.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.480
And there's a lot of value there.

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:17.400
People are more and more talking about Bitcoin
as a network that can support all sorts of

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:23.210
other interesting things that maybe money's
just the first application of this distributed

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:26.760
technology, where there's no central control.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:32.260
But there is this shared ledger of transactions,
where we all agree that these are the transactions

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:33.629
that took place.

00:08:33.629 --> 00:08:40.409
So people are, today, experimenting with using
it for all sorts of other different things.

00:08:40.409 --> 00:08:46.439
And so as we go forward, you may start hearing
about Bitcoin as, you know, not just currency,

00:08:46.439 --> 00:08:57.149
not just money, not even just a payment network;
but maybe -- maybe a way of signing title

00:08:57.149 --> 00:09:03.329
for houses or cars, or doing other kinds of
contracts.

00:09:03.329 --> 00:09:05.079
So there's all sorts of innovation that's
happening.

00:09:05.079 --> 00:09:09.910
And really, for me as a technology guy, that's
what makes me really excited.

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:15.110
It's all of the interesting things that are
being created on top of this new technology

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:21.850
that -- that, you know, keeps me awake at
night thinking about the possibilities.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:23.389
REDIKER: All right.

00:09:23.389 --> 00:09:28.700
So many questions; those I'd thought about
asking, and now some that just came up.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:30.680
But I'm not a tech guy.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:34.490
So as you could see, when I was trying to
raise the font on my iPad, I still have some

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:36.860
problems with the basics.

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:49.769
But if the software is designed in a way that
is reliable, dependable, open-source, et cetera,

00:09:49.769 --> 00:09:54.120
what is it that you do when you say, "I go
home, and I write more code for it"?

00:09:54.120 --> 00:10:02.389
In other words, are you constantly revising
the underlying computer code, and if so, why?

00:10:02.389 --> 00:10:05.949
Does that mean you're making it better, or
did you find flaws in it?

00:10:05.949 --> 00:10:11.610
And again, this isn't about Bitcoin in particular,
but just in general, if something is a currency

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:14.639
-- we'll get into what it is, or isn't, or
should be, or could be.

00:10:14.639 --> 00:10:20.649
But, you know, when you're the chief scientist,
and you're writing code for something, what

00:10:20.649 --> 00:10:22.170
does that mean?

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:23.189
ANDRESEN: Sure.

00:10:23.189 --> 00:10:27.029
I mean, I actually get asked that question
a lot.

00:10:27.029 --> 00:10:28.570
"Isn't Bitcoin finished?"

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:32.230
You know, "Why do you have to write any code?"

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:37.180
And being a software engineer, software's
never finished, right.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.180
There's always -- you can always optimize
it.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:41.610
You can always make it a little bit faster.

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:45.490
You can always make it a little bit easier
to use.

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:52.500
As Bitcoin grows, you know, a lot of what
I do, a lot of the work is scaling up, is

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:59.300
-- you know, when there were 10,000 people
trading Bitcoin back and forth, and there

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:05.939
were a few transactions happening every hour,
it's not hard to write software that handles

00:11:05.939 --> 00:11:07.959
a few transactions every hour.

00:11:07.959 --> 00:11:10.180
REDIKER: So like the Affordable Care Act had
some...

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:12.889
ANDRESEN: Well, they jumped into the deep
end, right.

00:11:12.889 --> 00:11:18.420
I mean, Bitcoin's had the luxury of kind of
organically growing at a very -- it's been

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:19.420
a steep path.

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:21.779
And it's been hard to keep up.

00:11:21.779 --> 00:11:26.279
But as Bitcoin continues to grow, we just
-- there's software engineering that has to

00:11:26.279 --> 00:11:31.939
be done to support thousands of transactions
per second, instead of dozens of transactions

00:11:31.939 --> 00:11:32.939
per day.

00:11:32.939 --> 00:11:35.690
REDIKER: So it's not changing the fundamentals.

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:39.940
It's simply allowing it to grow organically
or otherwise.

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:41.790
ANDRESEN: Pretty much, yes.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.170
I mean, yes.

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:44.170
Yes.

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:47.009
To the lay person, you know, it would look
like...

00:11:47.009 --> 00:11:48.009
(CROSSTALK)

00:11:48.009 --> 00:11:51.779
REDIKER: We're all lay people when it comes
to the technology underlying Bitcoin.

00:11:51.779 --> 00:11:55.730
ANDRESEN: It typically looks like we're doing
nothing, which is what we want, right.

00:11:55.730 --> 00:11:57.679
I mean, we want the system to just work.

00:11:57.679 --> 00:11:58.790
REDIKER: All right.

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:01.819
So let's -- the questions -- we spoke last
week.

00:12:01.819 --> 00:12:07.689
And I started with the basic skepticism of,
"It's a virtual currency," and we'll get into

00:12:07.689 --> 00:12:11.769
what a currency should be, could be, why we
need one, et cetera.

00:12:11.769 --> 00:12:20.319
But you pointed out it is both a virtual currency,
a payment system, or a payment network, and

00:12:20.319 --> 00:12:23.230
it's regulated in some quarters as a commodity.

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:27.660
So can you go through the -- and let's start
with the payment system, because I think that's

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:32.899
the least controversial, and the one that
I personally am the least skeptical about.

00:12:32.899 --> 00:12:38.089
I think it certainly adds value with limited
controversy.

00:12:38.089 --> 00:12:42.189
Can you just go through how it works as a
payment system, and what you think the need

00:12:42.189 --> 00:12:44.550
is that this is trying to address?

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:51.689
ANDRESEN: So if you think about the payment
systems we have today, you know, they're typically

00:12:51.689 --> 00:12:57.059
created back in pencil-and-paper days.

00:12:57.059 --> 00:13:01.860
So we have, you know, check-clearing payment
systems that have kind of evolved, and they

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:03.980
kind of work today.

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:09.700
Although, if you try to pay somebody, it might
take three or four, five days, depending on

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:13.929
whether there's a bank holiday in the middle
of, you know, when you wrote your check, when

00:13:13.929 --> 00:13:16.910
it actually gets to the other person's bank
account.

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:18.770
And that's kind of crazy, right.

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:25.860
I mean, I can send an e-mail across the world,
and it arrives in that person's inbox in five

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:29.540
seconds, 10 seconds.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:32.600
When you're talking about money these days,
I mean, you're talking about numbers in bank

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:33.699
accounts.

00:13:33.699 --> 00:13:41.820
Why does it take two, three, four, five days
to transfer value to somebody across the world?

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:46.269
The reason is, it evolved, right.

00:13:46.269 --> 00:13:50.879
It was not designed -- our payment systems
were not designed for the Internet.

00:13:50.879 --> 00:13:53.470
Bitcoin was designed for the Internet.

00:13:53.470 --> 00:14:02.989
It's fundamentally secure at a very low level,
using up-to-date cryptography, you know, using

00:14:02.989 --> 00:14:09.189
kind of best practices for how do you make
sure that the person you're paying is the

00:14:09.189 --> 00:14:12.910
person you're paying, and nobody can get in
the middle of that.

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:19.489
And so as a payment system, it's just much
more efficient than the payment systems we've

00:14:19.489 --> 00:14:21.850
had up to this point.

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:27.069
And because it's more efficient, it's cheaper,
faster, better.

00:14:27.069 --> 00:14:35.519
It also doesn't care about international boundaries,
which I mean, there's lots of regulations

00:14:35.519 --> 00:14:40.709
set up around national borders, which in the
age of the Internet, you really have to ask

00:14:40.709 --> 00:14:42.790
yourself, does that make sense anymore, right?

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:44.890
We're a globally-connected world.

00:14:44.890 --> 00:14:46.740
I can send e-mail across the world.

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:52.959
I don't have to go to the national post office,
and then put a stamp, that then goes to some

00:14:52.959 --> 00:14:54.449
other national post office.

00:14:54.449 --> 00:14:55.449
I just send e-mail.

00:14:55.449 --> 00:14:56.449
It just goes.

00:14:56.449 --> 00:14:57.449
It's the Internet.

00:14:57.449 --> 00:15:03.050
And so Bitcoin's designed for the Internet,
designed to be efficient.

00:15:03.050 --> 00:15:08.459
And so as a payment system, you know, lots
of people are seeing opportunities to make

00:15:08.459 --> 00:15:10.389
things better, cheaper, faster, for consumers.

00:15:10.389 --> 00:15:11.389
REDIKER: All right.

00:15:11.389 --> 00:15:14.990
So let's break that down for a minute.

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:21.070
So the existing payment system structure is
slow, inefficient, for, I would argue, many

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:26.170
reasons; one of which is, as you say, it was
written initially for the paper-and-pen crowd.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:30.499
And it has evolved in a slapdash kind of way.

00:15:30.499 --> 00:15:37.799
But the other is that there are actual impediments
set up for a reason, so regulators would say,

00:15:37.799 --> 00:15:43.929
"We have the need to know where funds are
coming from, where they're going to."

00:15:43.929 --> 00:15:52.739
There are banks that play the role of intermediaries,
both to make money, and also to abide by a

00:15:52.739 --> 00:15:57.649
regulatory or a legal framework that is both
country by country, and some international

00:15:57.649 --> 00:15:59.589
norms.

00:15:59.589 --> 00:16:07.519
Those could be seen as either the -- promoting
the common good, or as rent-seeking, and those

00:16:07.519 --> 00:16:13.199
intermediaries are simply trying to make their,
you know, 2 percent on somebody else's hard

00:16:13.199 --> 00:16:14.600
work.

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:18.759
But there's clearly some element of both involved
here.

00:16:18.759 --> 00:16:25.009
So whereas on the rent-seeking, you can argue
that Bitcoin and this kind of technology advance

00:16:25.009 --> 00:16:26.739
is a good thing.

00:16:26.739 --> 00:16:32.769
It does run the risk of allowing people to
bypass all the regulatory infrastructure that

00:16:32.769 --> 00:16:34.549
exists out there.

00:16:34.549 --> 00:16:39.799
And that leaves it prone to challenge that
its basically for people who want to avoid

00:16:39.799 --> 00:16:42.129
regulatory and legal oversight.

00:16:42.129 --> 00:16:47.480
We've seen those challenges play out both
in terms of the press, terms of skeptical

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:50.959
regulators, in terms of recent events we can
get into in a moment.

00:16:50.959 --> 00:16:57.459
But, you know, what's the pushback on, yes,
it's great, because it allows us to avoid

00:16:57.459 --> 00:17:04.299
all of the friction, when a lot of people
would say some friction is there for a reason?

00:17:04.299 --> 00:17:07.630
ANDRESEN: I may get myself in trouble.

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:11.150
REDIKER: Then I will have done my job this
morning.

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:15.650
ANDRESEN: Personally -- you know, personally,
I think regulation's always about weighing

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:22.110
risks, costs, and benefits.

00:17:22.110 --> 00:17:26.690
And so, you know, I think -- I'm not going
to tell regulators how to do their job.

00:17:26.690 --> 00:17:35.040
I would just encourage them to -- I think
regulators tend to focus on costs and risks,

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.760
and not to focus so much on benefits.

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:41.450
Although, I must say, I think, actually, the
regulators here in the U.S. have actually

00:17:41.450 --> 00:17:48.240
done a pretty good job of realizing that,
you know, there is a balance to be struck.

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:54.690
And if there are tremendous benefits, then
even if there are additional risks, if the

00:17:54.690 --> 00:18:02.430
benefits outweigh the risks, then the rational
thing to do is to, you know, allow -- or not

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:05.570
try to impede progress.

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:13.770
And so, yes, I obviously think the benefits
of Bitcoin are huge.

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:16.470
The potential is huge.

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:18.070
And that's why I'm working on it, right.

00:18:18.070 --> 00:18:25.390
I wouldn't work on it if I thought it was
going to make the world a horrible place.

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:30.780
Other people might disagree, although, so
far, I mean, it seems like most smart people

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:34.620
who've looked at Bitcoin really closely, they
see the value proposition.

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:37.090
They see that there are these huge benefits.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:45.940
And it will remain to be seen, you know, how
well regulators, governments, law enforcement,

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:46.940
adapt.

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:54.180
I think on a larger scale, going through this
with just the Internet in general, right,

00:18:54.180 --> 00:19:01.820
of how does government adapt to this kind
of new communication mechanism that lets people

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:07.620
all over the world maybe talk about things
that we wouldn't want them talking about,

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:10.770
that let's them interact in ways that they
weren't able to interact before.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:13.700
So like I said, I don't have the answers.

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:17.380
I think you just need to carefully look at
both the benefits and the costs.

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:22.200
REDIKER: So from your perspective then -- and
this is not meant to be definitive -- but

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:27.660
the idea of some regulatory mechanism is not
totally off the table.

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:32.021
So the idea of a bit license, I think some
regulators have talked about, we don't know

00:19:32.021 --> 00:19:34.710
what that would look like, and maybe you know.

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:37.820
But that's not a total nonstarter.

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:44.210
It's not all or nothing in terms of no government
having anything to do with it, or total clamp-down,

00:19:44.210 --> 00:19:48.860
as in the case of, I think China has prohibited
Bitcoin.

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:52.110
There's some middle ground that you think
might be an acceptable future?

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:57.130
ANDRESEN: I mean, for me personally, you know,
I think things like consumer protection make

00:19:57.130 --> 00:19:59.010
sense, you know.

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:06.391
You should have some idea of who you're interacting
with if you're trusting them with money, or

00:20:06.391 --> 00:20:10.610
whatever.

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:14.550
So things like that I think make a lot of
sense.

00:20:14.550 --> 00:20:22.860
Trying to control Bitcoin, I think a government
like North Korea will probably be very successful

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:26.520
in controlling Bitcoin, just in the same way
they've been successful in controlling the

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:29.160
access to the Internet.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:33.760
For other, you know, more liberal governments
around the world, it's going to be tough to

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:42.120
tell your citizens, you know, "Don't buy shoes
from somebody in this particular country,

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:44.300
because we don't like that country."

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:47.330
That's going to be hard to enforce.

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:54.650
So, you know, even though a regulator might
want to have that control, I'm not sure it's

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:55.650
even possible to...

00:20:55.650 --> 00:20:59.330
REDIKER: Well, the buying shoes, there's a
physical good that's being transferred.

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:02.620
So there are obviously -- just to use that
example, the alpaca socks.

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:06.060
They're coming across the border, they've
got to be physically transferred.

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:08.690
There's a means by which the government can
deal with that.

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:12.350
A transfer of funding, obviously, is the other
side of it.

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:14.930
And that's harder to control.

00:21:14.930 --> 00:21:20.920
So let me move on to the other aspect of this,
which is the virtual currency side.

00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:24.570
I think that's where I'm more skeptical.

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:31.250
Anything that goes from half a penny to $1,000
in a very short period of time, and then has

00:21:31.250 --> 00:21:36.400
a huge amount of volatility in the meantime,
gives you pause to question whether the basic

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:41.160
economic theory of what a currency is supposed
to be, store of value, medium of exchange,

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:47.320
and unit of account on the store of value
side, that leads people to say, "Well, hold

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:48.950
on now."

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:51.940
That's not just currency fluctuation.

00:21:51.940 --> 00:21:54.710
That's a pretty dramatic shift.

00:21:54.710 --> 00:22:01.090
Can you walk me through why we need a new
virtual currency -- not the payment system

00:22:01.090 --> 00:22:03.690
side -- but why we might need a new virtual
currency?

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:09.380
And then I'll probably push back on some of
the basics of what a central bank's role in

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:14.990
the world is, or ought to be, and whether
in fact something like Bitcoin is consistent

00:22:14.990 --> 00:22:16.050
with that.

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:19.930
ANDRESEN: Yes, it's interesting.

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:26.420
I mean, I'll get myself in trouble again,
this time, with a different audience.

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:31.000
If Bitcoin turns out to be a miserable failure,
which I should say, it's still possible.

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:38.820
It could be a miserable failure for technical
reasons, or sociological reasons, or monetary

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:39.820
reasons.

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:44.380
But if it's a miserable failure, but it does
encourage some government somewhere around

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:51.920
the world to realize that our payment systems
are really broken and archaic, and some country

00:22:51.920 --> 00:23:00.610
decides we're going to create a payment system
for this century that's -- where the currency

00:23:00.610 --> 00:23:05.500
is, you know, our national currency, I think
that would be a huge success.

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:13.110
Just that would add so much value to the world
of creating a way of -- it really doesn't

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:16.730
matter if the currency is dollars or euros,
or loonies.

00:23:16.730 --> 00:23:21.500
I think -- actually, the Canadian government's
actually been pretty -- done some interesting

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:25.190
experiments with putting Canadian dollars
in digital form.

00:23:25.190 --> 00:23:33.720
Or you can look at what's happening with M-Pesa
in Africa, very innovative payment system

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:36.440
with the Kenyan schilling being the backing
currency.

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:40.830
So if that's the only kind of effect that
Bitcoin has, as kind of showing the way, then

00:23:40.830 --> 00:23:44.110
I think, you know, Bitcoin will have been
a success.

00:23:44.110 --> 00:23:53.560
The argument for why Bitcoin, the currency,
there's several things that people bring up.

00:23:53.560 --> 00:23:56.350
So one is kind of the fixed supply, right.

00:23:56.350 --> 00:24:00.860
Everybody knows it's completely and utterly
predictable how many bitcoins there will be

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:01.860
next week.

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:07.480
You know, we know that they're being created
at a rate of 25 approximately every 10 minutes.

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:13.180
That rate is fixed in the code, and it, you
know, kind of self-adjusts.

00:24:13.180 --> 00:24:15.970
People all over the world are getting those
25 bitcoins.

00:24:15.970 --> 00:24:22.070
It's not, you know, one person issuing the
currency.

00:24:22.070 --> 00:24:27.680
And the economic theory is that if you know
that there's a fixed supply, and assuming

00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:33.020
that demand settles down, that, you know,
eventually everybody in the world will have

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:37.390
heard about Bitcoin, hopefully, and all the
people who want to use Bitcoin are using it,

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:39.920
then, you know, you have a predictable demand.

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:46.000
And if supply and demand are both reasonably
stable, then you get a stable price.

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:51.290
Because the price is purely driven by supply
and demand.

00:24:51.290 --> 00:24:55.330
Whether that's ever going to happen, nobody
really knows.

00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:59.040
Whether that's actually the correct economic
theory, you know, economists I think will

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:05.530
argue about endlessly, maybe for years and
years.

00:25:05.530 --> 00:25:08.320
I still say Bitcoin's an experiment.

00:25:08.320 --> 00:25:10.812
And it's an experiment in progress.

00:25:10.812 --> 00:25:13.120
And we'll have to see.

00:25:13.120 --> 00:25:18.740
But, you know, I mean, theoretically, if you
think about it, what if a government did decide

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:22.250
-- what if the Canadian government decided,
you know, "Our currency is going to be a virtual

00:25:22.250 --> 00:25:23.290
currency.

00:25:23.290 --> 00:25:28.230
We're going to, you know, upgrade our payment
system, so anybody anywhere in the world can

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:36.730
use it," would other countries trust the Canadian
government to do the right thing, to always

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:40.080
uphold their end of the bargain?

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:45.230
I think the U.S. government would probably
have a big issue with kind of saying to its

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:48.410
citizens, "Yes, you guys should start using
this cool currency the Canadian government

00:25:48.410 --> 00:25:49.720
invented."

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:57.160
So a currency like Bitcoin, where there's
nobody in charge, there's no country in charge,

00:25:57.160 --> 00:26:03.350
there's no corporation in charge, it's everybody
who's using the currency is supporting the

00:26:03.350 --> 00:26:12.310
currency, theoretically might be something
that everybody could agree, since there's

00:26:12.310 --> 00:26:15.470
no one person in charge, "Yes, this is something
that we could all use."

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:18.700
REDIKER: So I'm going to push back.

00:26:18.700 --> 00:26:23.630
First of all, the fixed demand is a question
I would push back on, because not only is

00:26:23.630 --> 00:26:29.780
there not fixed demand in any given quarter,
month, year, but over time, the population

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:35.680
of the earth grows, and economic growth continues
to hopefully be a dynamic.

00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:42.570
So over time, you do have both volatility
in demand, but a steady, upward trend in demand.

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:47.150
And if you cap the amount of supply, then
by definition, you're going to have an imbalance

00:26:47.150 --> 00:26:51.600
at some point in the future, which is why
there are those who are skeptical for, amongst

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:59.040
other reasons, for the inherent deflationary
aspects of Bitcoin; meaning that if Bitcoin

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:04.520
continues to go up in value, because you've
got a fixed supply, and a potentially increasing

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:09.920
demand over the long term, then by definition,
if the value of the currency goes up, then

00:27:09.920 --> 00:27:12.750
the value of what it can buy goes down.

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:18.470
And therefore, you have deflation, which,
you know, in the generation of those in this

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:22.940
room, is somewhat of a new phenomenon that
we're talking about in Europe right now, the

00:27:22.940 --> 00:27:30.090
risks of deflation; the Fed in the U.S. having
fought off the risks of deflation in the post-financial-crisis

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:31.500
era.

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:37.890
So it's one thing to say this potentially
takes care of your inflationary problems.

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:46.550
But don't central banks play a valuable role
in using all of their economic and discretionary

00:27:46.550 --> 00:27:52.820
tools to actually adjust both interest rates
and the supply of money, precisely because

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:58.930
the world is a messy place, and things don't
always go as a software program might predict

00:27:58.930 --> 00:28:00.540
that they should?

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:04.850
And you need this human element in there,
precisely as we've seen over the past several

00:28:04.850 --> 00:28:11.190
years, to adjust the money supply and interest
rates, to take account of what's going on

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:13.100
in the real world.

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:14.940
ANDRESEN: Yes.

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:17.700
I don't know.

00:28:17.700 --> 00:28:25.850
I mean, if you talk to most Bitcoiners, they
would say, no, central banks have done a horrible

00:28:25.850 --> 00:28:26.850
job.

00:28:26.850 --> 00:28:30.690
In my wallet, I actually keep some foreign
currency.

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:39.590
I keep a $100 trillion Reserve note from Zimbabwe
-- it was issued in 2009 -- which is, you

00:28:39.590 --> 00:28:47.350
know, that's an extreme example of a central
bank screwing up, either on purpose or accidentally.

00:28:47.350 --> 00:28:51.890
As a technology person, you know, deflation
doesn't bother me, right.

00:28:51.890 --> 00:28:56.650
That iPad that you're holding, you couldn't
buy it 10 years ago.

00:28:56.650 --> 00:29:00.870
If you could buy it, I don't know how many
millions of dollars it would cost, right.

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:06.850
We certainly have rapid deflation in the high-tech
world, and just in terms of the things we

00:29:06.850 --> 00:29:08.100
can buy.

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:10.200
We seem to do OK with that, you know.

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:14.560
I mean, Apple seems to be doing OK, even though
you know next year there's going to be a fancier

00:29:14.560 --> 00:29:17.190
version of that, that you're going to want.

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:20.560
So I'm not an economist.

00:29:20.560 --> 00:29:27.670
You know, I know that there's all sorts of
theories about sticky wages and, how could

00:29:27.670 --> 00:29:33.800
you write contracts in a currency if it's
deflationary?

00:29:33.800 --> 00:29:35.340
Again, I don't know.

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:37.760
Bitcoin's an experiment.

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:41.440
I think these are questions that, if Bitcoin
continues to be successful, maybe it will

00:29:41.440 --> 00:29:47.810
have to worry about in 20, 30, 40 years -- I
don't know what the world is going to be like

00:29:47.810 --> 00:29:52.590
in 20, 30, 40 years.

00:29:52.590 --> 00:29:56.650
But they're certainly fascinating, interesting
questions, I think are going to be debated

00:29:56.650 --> 00:29:57.930
for quite a while.

00:29:57.930 --> 00:30:02.840
REDIKER: Speaking of fascinating and interesting
questions, it is at the witching hour of 9

00:30:02.840 --> 00:30:06.980
o'clock, which means I'm going to open it
up to the audience for your fascinating and

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:07.980
interesting questions.

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:12.540
So I think I've got something I was supposed
to say here.

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:14.460
Hold on, let me see.

00:30:14.460 --> 00:30:15.460
Right.

00:30:15.460 --> 00:30:16.460
Please wait for the microphone.

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:17.460
Speak directly into it.

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:19.480
Please stand and say your name and affiliation.

00:30:19.480 --> 00:30:24.120
Keep your questions and comments concise to
allow as many attendees as possible to speak.

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:25.340
So I'm going to start right here.

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:28.240
Wait for the mike, please.

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:30.530
QUESTION: Hi, Danny Frifeld (ph).

00:30:30.530 --> 00:30:36.371
I'm sorry if I missed this, but I'm having
a hard time extricating the payment mechanism

00:30:36.371 --> 00:30:38.010
from the underlying instrument.

00:30:38.010 --> 00:30:40.350
I get the attractiveness of the mechanism.

00:30:40.350 --> 00:30:45.480
But what is the -- if the underlying instrument
has some attractiveness, and now it's got

00:30:45.480 --> 00:30:49.650
fixed demand -- fixed supply, rather -- and
so it's a store value, or it provides some

00:30:49.650 --> 00:30:54.650
predictability, that it's not going to be
subject to such rampant inflationary pressures,

00:30:54.650 --> 00:30:55.990
that it'll lose all value.

00:30:55.990 --> 00:31:01.910
But why – again -- notwithstanding what
the central bank may do wrong in this country,

00:31:01.910 --> 00:31:05.530
but the dollar is effectively serving that
purpose, along with a couple other currencies.

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:12.380
So I know you've alluded to, if the one contribution
Bitcoin made to humanity was dragging along

00:31:12.380 --> 00:31:15.710
the improvement of payment mechanisms around
the world.

00:31:15.710 --> 00:31:21.120
But I still -- what I'm sort of missing, is
that, you know, there are other systems, again,

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:23.830
all rent-seeking, like credit cards and PayPal
and the like.

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:28.020
But what's really the attractiveness of the
instrument behind this as a store of value,

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:31.660
beyond something like gold or the dollar?

00:31:31.660 --> 00:31:36.690
ANDRESEN: I think the attractiveness is the
fixed supply, right.

00:31:36.690 --> 00:31:41.920
If you know that there will only ever be 21
million bitcoins in the world, and you have

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:48.720
one of them, I mean, you have one-twenty-one-millionth
of that commodity.

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:52.330
And nobody can take it away from you.

00:31:52.330 --> 00:31:54.740
And that's another key part of Bitcoin.

00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:58.400
If you own bitcoins, you actually own them.

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:00.470
It's like cash in that way.

00:32:00.470 --> 00:32:09.270
They exist as these long numbers, essentially,
that can't be taken away from you.

00:32:09.270 --> 00:32:14.740
Nobody -- until you sign a transaction that
transfers the value somewhere else, you own

00:32:14.740 --> 00:32:15.740
them.

00:32:15.740 --> 00:32:18.840
You can print them out on a piece of paper,
and put them in your safe-deposit box.

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:25.830
I actually have a bitcoin in my wallet that
I can show you, that, you know, has that number

00:32:25.830 --> 00:32:28.380
on it.

00:32:28.380 --> 00:32:32.490
And so if you're looking for some other store
of value, something like gold works, right.

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:34.440
If you own gold, you actually do own it.

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:41.230
It's, you know, sitting -- you can hide it
under your mattress.

00:32:41.230 --> 00:32:45.100
But it's not convenient to trade, right.

00:32:45.100 --> 00:32:51.460
And so Bitcoin combines a really interesting
-- it's both a commodity that you really do

00:32:51.460 --> 00:32:54.210
own when you own bitcoins.

00:32:54.210 --> 00:32:57.440
But it's also incredibly convenient to trade,
because it's, you know, a creature of the

00:32:57.440 --> 00:32:58.440
Internet.

00:32:58.440 --> 00:33:02.900
If I want to send a bitcoin, sit down at my
computer, sign a transaction, send it anywhere

00:33:02.900 --> 00:33:03.970
in the world.

00:33:03.970 --> 00:33:12.730
So I think that's, as a store of value, as
a, you know, a commodity, as a token, that's

00:33:12.730 --> 00:33:14.640
why so many people find it valuable.

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:17.330
REDIKER: I just picked up on this.

00:33:17.330 --> 00:33:23.460
I saw a study last night as I was preparing
for this, saying that 64 percent of all bitcoins

00:33:23.460 --> 00:33:27.179
are hoarded, or are not used.

00:33:27.179 --> 00:33:32.950
So just as a make-believe economist, let me
just throw out that the purpose of currency

00:33:32.950 --> 00:33:38.050
is to be used, so that they are used to stimulate
economic activity, which feeds growth and

00:33:38.050 --> 00:33:40.490
jobs, and all that good stuff.

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:46.860
So if the limited supply means you know it's
going to increase in value over time, you're

00:33:46.860 --> 00:33:53.320
not getting into deflation versus inflation,
but the fact that I know I've got it doesn't

00:33:53.320 --> 00:33:59.230
actually help the broader, global economy,
because I'm not using it to stimulate economic

00:33:59.230 --> 00:34:03.460
activity, which ought to be a pretty fundamental
tenet of why you use a currency.

00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:09.690
ANDRESEN: I mean, in theory, that's true.

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:12.860
In practice, it doesn't seem to work out that
way.

00:34:12.860 --> 00:34:17.010
So BitPay is a large Bitcoin payment processor.

00:34:17.010 --> 00:34:19.140
You can think of them as like the PayPal for
Bitcoin.

00:34:19.140 --> 00:34:24.340
They help merchants accept payments for goods
and services in Bitcoin.

00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:29.580
And they tell me that as the Bitcoin price
rises, they see more transactions.

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:31.530
So people are doing exactly the opposite.

00:34:31.530 --> 00:34:36.310
And it might just be a wealth effect, where,
"I bought some bitcoins for $100, they're

00:34:36.310 --> 00:34:38.270
now worth $200 apiece.

00:34:38.270 --> 00:34:43.611
I have $100 I can spend, right, without -- I
might as well spend it.

00:34:43.611 --> 00:34:44.990
It's basically free money."

00:34:44.990 --> 00:34:47.791
And I think you might see some of that.

00:34:47.791 --> 00:34:54.361
And I think that's something that people will
want to hold onto bitcoins as kind of -- eventually,

00:34:54.361 --> 00:34:59.560
the idea would be to hold onto bitcoins as
a safe store of value, just like you might

00:34:59.560 --> 00:35:06.040
put, you know, cash under your mattress if
you don't trust the banks, if you don't trust

00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:07.040
anybody else.

00:35:07.040 --> 00:35:11.960
It's generally a bad idea, cash under the
mattress, in case you have a house fire, whatever.

00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:22.090
Bitcoins could function as a high-tech, easier
to kind of back up cash under the mattress.

00:35:22.090 --> 00:35:25.070
And I think there will always be some of that.

00:35:25.070 --> 00:35:26.100
You could call that hoarding.

00:35:26.100 --> 00:35:31.040
You can call that saving -- savings.

00:35:31.040 --> 00:35:36.000
But if the price goes high enough, and there's
something you want to buy, I think you will

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:42.780
-- certainly, I paid for my hotel room here,
actually, using Bitcoin.

00:35:42.780 --> 00:35:49.710
And was very happy to do it, and actually
saved a little bit of money, because the company

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:55.550
that's doing it saves on credit-card fees
when they use Bitcoin instead of...

00:35:55.550 --> 00:35:57.880
So, you know, we'll see.

00:35:57.880 --> 00:36:00.660
In practice, it doesn't seem to be an issue.

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:06.850
QUESTION: I was told to stand.

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:08.030
My name is Carter Dougherty.

00:36:08.030 --> 00:36:09.330
I'm with Bloomberg News.

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:14.820
I'm a resident bitcoiniac, bitcoin boy, call
me what you will.

00:36:14.820 --> 00:36:19.000
I think it's safe to say that when you look
at these functions of the store of value versus

00:36:19.000 --> 00:36:23.810
the medium of exchange, the area that's attracted
the most attention in the sense of venture

00:36:23.810 --> 00:36:28.161
capital, and serious investment, has been
the medium-of-exchange concept, whether you're

00:36:28.161 --> 00:36:33.980
looking at Coinbase, or Circle Financial in
Boston, that sort of thing.

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:40.580
And that's really what attracts the people
who thought about this for a long time, because

00:36:40.580 --> 00:36:46.290
Bitcoin shows all the signs of being a disruptive
payment system that doesn't simply piggyback

00:36:46.290 --> 00:36:52.050
on the existing system, which, if you think
about it, is really what PayPal has done.

00:36:52.050 --> 00:36:59.210
Do you think that over time, if the sort of
commercial evolution is in that direction,

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:06.100
of this focus on the medium of exchange, will
the enthusiasts, the ur-enthusiasts, the developers,

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:11.670
the engineers, the ideas people, will they
maintain their interests and focus on Bitcoin

00:37:11.670 --> 00:37:13.100
and digital currency?

00:37:13.100 --> 00:37:17.390
Because there is -- we all heard the sort
of disdain in your voice when you mentioned

00:37:17.390 --> 00:37:18.390
central banks.

00:37:18.390 --> 00:37:23.420
I mean, this is a widespread kind of libertarian
bent of many bitcoiniacs.

00:37:23.420 --> 00:37:31.070
Would that -- is the enthusiasm going to continue
if we move in this other direction?

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:35.020
ANDRESEN: I think so.

00:37:35.020 --> 00:37:40.760
We'll see.

00:37:40.760 --> 00:37:45.320
There's -- I mean, there's so much potential
with Bitcoin.

00:37:45.320 --> 00:37:51.830
I mean, we're really -- like I said, I get
excited about all the interesting things that

00:37:51.830 --> 00:37:53.690
can be done with this new technology, right.

00:37:53.690 --> 00:37:58.869
I'm a technology guy, so I'm always thinking
about what's going to be next.

00:37:58.869 --> 00:38:07.369
And so people, like venture capitalists, will
look at just kind of the meat and potatoes,

00:38:07.369 --> 00:38:10.820
you know, "What industries that are already
incredibly profitable can we disrupt?

00:38:10.820 --> 00:38:17.750
How can we get a piece of that trillion-dollar
industry, and make a bunch of money?"

00:38:17.750 --> 00:38:24.970
That's not that interesting to me, personally.

00:38:24.970 --> 00:38:27.600
I think there's enough kind of meat there.

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:34.880
Bitcoin's such an interesting kind of revolutionary
disruptive technology, that I think it will

00:38:34.880 --> 00:38:40.020
keep people's interest, even while we have,
you know, lots of money being thrown at kind

00:38:40.020 --> 00:38:47.030
of how do you optimize the existing infrastructure
and payment system, and work in the existing

00:38:47.030 --> 00:38:48.030
system?

00:38:48.030 --> 00:38:49.090
So I think we're going to have both.

00:38:49.090 --> 00:38:54.820
You know, as chief scientist at the Bitcoin
Foundation, kind of my -- part of my job is

00:38:54.820 --> 00:39:02.940
to encourage lots of different experiments
in lots of different areas, and to see, you

00:39:02.940 --> 00:39:07.290
know -- well, to make sure that the fundamental
technology keeps chugging away, processing

00:39:07.290 --> 00:39:13.200
transactions reliably, and then to let, you
know, all this wonderful innovation and experimentation

00:39:13.200 --> 00:39:15.230
happen on top.

00:39:15.230 --> 00:39:16.680
So...

00:39:16.680 --> 00:39:20.210
REDIKER: In the back.

00:39:20.210 --> 00:39:22.720
QUESTION: Hi, John Stubbs.

00:39:22.720 --> 00:39:24.130
You touched on a few of these.

00:39:24.130 --> 00:39:29.390
But I was just wondering what you see as the
biggest, or most significant, existential

00:39:29.390 --> 00:39:34.390
threat to Bitcoin in the next two to three
years?

00:39:34.390 --> 00:39:44.920
ANDRESEN: I have a deep respect for, you know,
threats that are like an asteroid that you

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:49.070
didn't know was there, coming, and like wiping
out the dinosaurs, you know.

00:39:49.070 --> 00:39:53.600
So I always have trouble answering this question,
because I think the biggest -- probably the

00:39:53.600 --> 00:39:59.330
biggest threat is something that just isn't
on my radar.

00:39:59.330 --> 00:40:02.920
I have no idea.

00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:07.300
You know, maybe there's somebody working in
his basement that has a system that's 10 times

00:40:07.300 --> 00:40:09.750
better than Bitcoin, and will come out with
it.

00:40:09.750 --> 00:40:13.250
And you know, just -- everybody says, "Oh,
that's way better.

00:40:13.250 --> 00:40:15.150
We'll just use that instead of Bitcoin."

00:40:15.150 --> 00:40:16.150
It's possible.

00:40:16.150 --> 00:40:18.950
You know, that's -- I don't think it's likely.

00:40:18.950 --> 00:40:19.950
But that's possible.

00:40:19.950 --> 00:40:27.471
It's possible that society in general decides
Bitcoin's a bad idea; that, you know, maybe

00:40:27.471 --> 00:40:33.860
crazy, high-tech people think it's the greatest
thing ever.

00:40:33.860 --> 00:40:36.270
Ordinary people just think it's unpatriotic.

00:40:36.270 --> 00:40:39.670
They think it's -- whatever.

00:40:39.670 --> 00:40:48.450
You know, that could happen, and that could,
you know, make it basically be a tiny little

00:40:48.450 --> 00:40:55.450
niche that's only used by maybe unsavory people,
and it fails, because nobody wants to be associated

00:40:55.450 --> 00:41:01.080
with something that's only used for unsavory
purposes.

00:41:01.080 --> 00:41:03.580
A technical glitch is certainly possible.

00:41:03.580 --> 00:41:12.100
As we scale up the Bitcoin network, I get
kept up at night worrying that we're going

00:41:12.100 --> 00:41:19.070
to introduce some bug in the code that, you
know, maybe we need to deploy some software

00:41:19.070 --> 00:41:20.580
that everybody uses.

00:41:20.580 --> 00:41:23.890
And then, unbeknownst to us, there's some
bug that brings the network down for a day

00:41:23.890 --> 00:41:24.890
or two.

00:41:24.890 --> 00:41:29.530
That could really shake confidence in this
payment system; that, you know, a payment

00:41:29.530 --> 00:41:33.650
system that goes down for a couple days is
not a payment system you want to rely on.

00:41:33.650 --> 00:41:38.670
Although, I guess there are existing banking
payment systems, kind of go down every weekend.

00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:42.320
So maybe that's not an existential threat.

00:41:42.320 --> 00:41:45.330
But, you know, those are the kinds of things
that I think about.

00:41:45.330 --> 00:41:50.970
But again, asteroid killing the dinosaurs
might be out there that I just don't know

00:41:50.970 --> 00:41:51.970
about.

00:41:51.970 --> 00:41:53.830
QUESTION: Thank you.

00:41:53.830 --> 00:41:58.420
Ornstein Mallock (ph) from the State Department.

00:41:58.420 --> 00:42:00.090
Quick question.

00:42:00.090 --> 00:42:04.790
For technology to really go mass, you normally
need it to be elegant.

00:42:04.790 --> 00:42:09.000
So Bitcoin may be mathematically, and from
a cryptography perspective, very elegant,

00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:10.520
from a user perspective.

00:42:10.520 --> 00:42:13.050
So one bitcoin, for instance, costs $800.

00:42:13.050 --> 00:42:16.830
So if the average person on the street wants
to get a bitcoin, it's very expensive.

00:42:16.830 --> 00:42:19.480
And so you need to have smaller denominations.

00:42:19.480 --> 00:42:22.910
And I know that right now, people buy zero-point-zero-zero-zero
-- whatever -- bitcoin.

00:42:22.910 --> 00:42:24.869
That's not very elegant.

00:42:24.869 --> 00:42:28.390
And it has to be like the iPad, something
which is, you know, user-friendly.

00:42:28.390 --> 00:42:33.760
Is there some effort being made to make it
more elegant, more user-friendly in that regard?

00:42:33.760 --> 00:42:34.760
ANDRESEN: Absolutely.

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:42.020
I mean, a part of the excitement in Silicon
Valley is, you know, startups that are trying

00:42:42.020 --> 00:42:48.290
to make really easy ways for people to buy
Bitcoin, easy ways for people to trade Bitcoin,

00:42:48.290 --> 00:42:51.369
easy ways for people to make their Bitcoin
secure.

00:42:51.369 --> 00:42:56.270
I saw, I think just yesterday, the day before,
there's announcement of a little wrist thing

00:42:56.270 --> 00:42:59.570
that can hold your bitcoins.

00:42:59.570 --> 00:43:02.170
And it monitors your heart.

00:43:02.170 --> 00:43:05.570
And apparently, our heartbeats are unique.

00:43:05.570 --> 00:43:09.490
And so, you know, somebody steals your wrist
thing, they can't steal your bitcoins, because

00:43:09.490 --> 00:43:14.340
they don't have your same heartbeat, which
is wild and crazy.

00:43:14.340 --> 00:43:18.250
And I don't know if that will succeed or not,
but that's an example of kind of one of the

00:43:18.250 --> 00:43:24.500
experiments of what people are doing to make
it really easy for people to use Bitcoin to

00:43:24.500 --> 00:43:26.310
keep them secure.

00:43:26.310 --> 00:43:27.360
And we will see that.

00:43:27.360 --> 00:43:33.500
I mean, you're right.

00:43:33.500 --> 00:43:37.730
Buying -- there's this idea that you have
to buy one bitcoin, which is not true.

00:43:37.730 --> 00:43:40.510
You can buy one-thousandth of the bitcoin.

00:43:40.510 --> 00:43:46.790
I think we'll see kind of, from a bottom-up,
emergent process.

00:43:46.790 --> 00:43:50.790
We might start talking about milli bitcoins,
millies, instead of bitcoins.

00:43:50.790 --> 00:43:57.550
So I'll send you two millies for that cup
of coffee, or whatever.

00:43:57.550 --> 00:43:58.550
That's going to be a bottom-up.

00:43:58.550 --> 00:43:59.550
I think that'll be a bottom-up process.

00:43:59.550 --> 00:44:04.750
I don't know, you could imagine the Bitcoin
Foundation taking the lead in saying, "We

00:44:04.750 --> 00:44:11.180
think that this nomenclature shall be the
nomenclature," although, that's really not

00:44:11.180 --> 00:44:12.180
in the spirit of the bitcoin.

00:44:12.180 --> 00:44:18.120
The spirit of Bitcoin is more to kind of let
that kind of thing emerge.

00:44:18.120 --> 00:44:26.820
QUESTION: Marvin Amore (ph), the New America
Foundation.

00:44:26.820 --> 00:44:30.590
There's a lot of excitement in the human-rights
community around Bitcoin.

00:44:30.590 --> 00:44:36.020
And I know that WordPress.com allows bloggers
around the world who might be anonymous to

00:44:36.020 --> 00:44:37.320
pay with Bitcoin.

00:44:37.320 --> 00:44:41.740
So I'm wondering if you've seen anything interesting
around human rights and Bitcoin.

00:44:41.740 --> 00:44:46.660
And also, since we're at the Council on Foreign
Relations, any thoughts on foreign policy

00:44:46.660 --> 00:44:48.880
and Bitcoin, sort of foreign aid?

00:44:48.880 --> 00:44:52.609
Anything you could write in the code to constrain
or monitor where American foreign aid might

00:44:52.609 --> 00:44:56.100
go, if given in Bitcoin, or another altcoin?

00:44:56.100 --> 00:44:57.150
ANDRESEN: Yes.

00:44:57.150 --> 00:45:06.180
I mean, you know, on the benefit side, you
know, bringing kind of a stable payment network

00:45:06.180 --> 00:45:11.280
to people in the world that really haven't
had that, you know, a stable, modern payment

00:45:11.280 --> 00:45:19.120
network, to anybody who has, you know, a smartphone,
or even a dumb phone -- I think smartphones,

00:45:19.120 --> 00:45:22.030
you know, in five years, I think pretty much
everybody in the world will have a smartphone,

00:45:22.030 --> 00:45:23.030
right.

00:45:23.030 --> 00:45:24.619
That's just the process of technology, right.

00:45:24.619 --> 00:45:26.650
iPhones are expensive.

00:45:26.650 --> 00:45:32.590
Fifteen years from now, you'll have an iPhone
given away in your Cheerios, as, you know,

00:45:32.590 --> 00:45:34.180
your little toy.

00:45:34.180 --> 00:45:36.720
It's the wonderful thing about technology.

00:45:36.720 --> 00:45:40.510
It just gets cheaper.

00:45:40.510 --> 00:45:44.840
And so I think there is huge opportunity there,
and that there are people, you know, working

00:45:44.840 --> 00:45:54.070
hard on making Bitcoin usable in places in
the world that don't have a lot of infrastructure.

00:45:54.070 --> 00:45:58.220
And so there's a lot of -- I think there will
be a lot of innovation happening there.

00:45:58.220 --> 00:45:59.220
And that's fantastic.

00:45:59.220 --> 00:46:04.030
And it'll be even more fantastic if the people
doing that innovation are the people in those

00:46:04.030 --> 00:46:05.210
countries.

00:46:05.210 --> 00:46:08.000
I want to see a lot more of that.

00:46:08.000 --> 00:46:15.730
For foreign aid, I don't know much about the
foreign-aid world.

00:46:15.730 --> 00:46:20.330
I mean, one of the interesting things about
Bitcoin, is it's this distributed ledger,

00:46:20.330 --> 00:46:28.180
where if you want, you can be incredibly transparent
about, you know, "These are the bitcoins that

00:46:28.180 --> 00:46:30.540
are coming in, and these are the bitcoins
going out."

00:46:30.540 --> 00:46:35.770
And we have this data structure called the
"block chain," where anybody can audit it.

00:46:35.770 --> 00:46:42.430
So, you know, if you publish, "These are all
my bitcoin transactions that I got in, these

00:46:42.430 --> 00:46:46.730
are all the bitcoin transactions that I got
out, that went out," and, you know, "This

00:46:46.730 --> 00:46:52.030
is my bitcoin balance," you know, anybody
can audit that, and you don't need to go visit

00:46:52.030 --> 00:46:55.890
some corporate offices, and pour through records.

00:46:55.890 --> 00:46:57.740
It's out there in the public.

00:46:57.740 --> 00:47:03.440
So I think that's actually part of Bitcoin
that hasn't been explored much at all, is

00:47:03.440 --> 00:47:11.550
how do you -- how can you use it as a very
transparent payment system, so that a non-profit

00:47:11.550 --> 00:47:17.520
can be completely upfront with their finances,
and everybody can trust that the money is

00:47:17.520 --> 00:47:20.980
actually going to what they say it's going
to.

00:47:20.980 --> 00:47:24.480
I just want to throw one other thing.

00:47:24.480 --> 00:47:31.750
I was really excited last week to give money
to a charity called, "Give Directly," which

00:47:31.750 --> 00:47:34.540
has the simplest model in the world for a
charity.

00:47:34.540 --> 00:47:37.580
They find poor people, and they give them
money.

00:47:37.580 --> 00:47:39.690
That's it.

00:47:39.690 --> 00:47:40.870
Incredibly low overhead.

00:47:40.870 --> 00:47:48.180
I believe they're operating in Kenya -- Kenya
and Uganda at the moment.

00:47:48.180 --> 00:47:53.380
And they just -- I asked them if they would
accept bitcoin donations, and actually got

00:47:53.380 --> 00:48:01.130
to beta-test their bitcoin donation process,
and gave them some bitcoins, that they will

00:48:01.130 --> 00:48:06.320
then turn around, and give directly to poor
people in Africa, which I think is just a

00:48:06.320 --> 00:48:13.690
fantastic -- and, you know, they're turning
it into cash, and they're actually transferring

00:48:13.690 --> 00:48:21.420
that cash overseas, and turning it into Kenyan
schillings, and then it goes, Kenyan schillings

00:48:21.420 --> 00:48:25.190
to the M-Pesa system, which works on, you
know, phones.

00:48:25.190 --> 00:48:28.750
If the poor person doesn't actually have a
cheap cell phone, they'll actually give them

00:48:28.750 --> 00:48:31.290
a phone, so that they can receive the money.

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:36.340
Then they have it basically on their phone.

00:48:36.340 --> 00:48:39.859
And all of that process, there's all sorts
of friction there.

00:48:39.859 --> 00:48:41.550
But we could do a lot better, right.

00:48:41.550 --> 00:48:45.720
It would be much better if I could donate
bitcoins to them, and then they just turn

00:48:45.720 --> 00:48:52.140
around and send the bitcoins directly to the
poor farmers in Africa.

00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:53.560
And then they'd trade them amongst themselves.

00:48:53.560 --> 00:48:54.570
That's the vision.

00:48:54.570 --> 00:48:56.070
And that's where I hope we get.

00:48:56.070 --> 00:49:01.480
And that's the kind of thing that, you know,
makes me excited, makes me want to work on

00:49:01.480 --> 00:49:02.480
Bitcoin more.

00:49:02.480 --> 00:49:04.840
REDIKER: Right here in the middle.

00:49:04.840 --> 00:49:09.070
QUESTION: Hi, Sean Goldheart (ph) of Barclays.

00:49:09.070 --> 00:49:11.150
So on the side, I run a charity.

00:49:11.150 --> 00:49:13.760
And we get virtual currency, not Bitcoin.

00:49:13.760 --> 00:49:14.760
Well, at least, not yet.

00:49:14.760 --> 00:49:17.180
But if you guys are giving more out, we'd
love to take it.

00:49:17.180 --> 00:49:21.960
It's been great, because we are able to get
cash for it, computers for our kids, suits

00:49:21.960 --> 00:49:25.700
for the children, whatever we need, which,
it's always interesting, because when a new

00:49:25.700 --> 00:49:28.930
vendor comes on the system, we get very excited,
because it's somewhere new we can spend our

00:49:28.930 --> 00:49:29.930
money.

00:49:29.930 --> 00:49:32.570
And I think of Bitcoin now as like American
Express points.

00:49:32.570 --> 00:49:34.630
You can use it in New York City cabs.

00:49:34.630 --> 00:49:35.630
You can trade it for cash.

00:49:35.630 --> 00:49:37.740
You can trade it for a flight or an iPad.

00:49:37.740 --> 00:49:42.260
How much time do you guys spend trying to
find new vendors, versus vendors coming to

00:49:42.260 --> 00:49:45.220
you, because they see this as like a popular
item of exchange?

00:49:45.220 --> 00:49:49.010
Because the way -- I don't -- I think if an
asteroid's going to come hit the earth, in

00:49:49.010 --> 00:49:50.520
my opinion, nobody's going to want Bitcoin.

00:49:50.520 --> 00:49:53.680
You're going to want a military behind your
dollar.

00:49:53.680 --> 00:49:57.460
And a bunch of guys in Silicon Valley are
not going to protect me.

00:49:57.460 --> 00:50:02.480
So I see Bitcoin having a limited -- not a
limited -- having a use as the ability, like

00:50:02.480 --> 00:50:04.470
American Express points are to buy certain
things.

00:50:04.470 --> 00:50:06.920
But it only works if a lot of vendors accept
it.

00:50:06.920 --> 00:50:10.170
And it feels like now more and more vendors
are beginning to accept Bitcoin.

00:50:10.170 --> 00:50:14.340
So what's the effort you guys are making to
find more vendors to accept it, to kind of

00:50:14.340 --> 00:50:16.550
ward off these other virtual currencies?

00:50:16.550 --> 00:50:23.540
ANDRESEN: Well, you say, "you guys," which
is not correct.

00:50:23.540 --> 00:50:25.599
There is no "you guys" with Bitcoin.

00:50:25.599 --> 00:50:27.099
With Bitcoin, it's everybody.

00:50:27.099 --> 00:50:31.420
It could be you.

00:50:31.420 --> 00:50:37.790
There's no central -- I mean, the Bitcoin
Foundation exists as a trade organization

00:50:37.790 --> 00:50:43.530
to talk to regulators, to give information
to people who, like, are trying to get their

00:50:43.530 --> 00:50:48.910
hands around this weird, decentralized system.

00:50:48.910 --> 00:50:53.410
So I know, for example, the folks at BitPay
and Coinbase, which are two bitcoin -- big

00:50:53.410 --> 00:50:58.120
bitcoin companies here in the United States,
they spend a lot of time talking to merchants,

00:50:58.120 --> 00:50:59.120
you know.

00:50:59.120 --> 00:51:03.660
I know -- I don't remember if it was BitPay
or Coinbase, that did the Overstock.com deal.

00:51:03.660 --> 00:51:04.660
Coinbase?

00:51:04.660 --> 00:51:05.660
Coinbase.

00:51:05.660 --> 00:51:12.730
So they spent time, you know, talking to Overstock.com
CEO, and selling it to him, and getting Overstock.com

00:51:12.730 --> 00:51:15.440
to accept bitcoin.

00:51:15.440 --> 00:51:17.369
That's being replicated all over the world.

00:51:17.369 --> 00:51:26.060
So entrepreneurs are kind of -- people will
send me e-mails saying, "I would like to do

00:51:26.060 --> 00:51:27.750
this or that with Bitcoin.

00:51:27.750 --> 00:51:28.750
May I?"

00:51:28.750 --> 00:51:33.740
And my response is, "You don't have to ask
anybody's permission or advice."

00:51:33.740 --> 00:51:34.740
It's an open system.

00:51:34.740 --> 00:51:42.140
Anybody can jump in and do whatever they want
with it, subject to whatever regulations exist

00:51:42.140 --> 00:51:43.770
in your jurisdiction.

00:51:43.770 --> 00:51:48.830
That's, you know, part of what some of the
early entrepreneurs are finding, is that there

00:51:48.830 --> 00:51:52.680
are things that you -- just because you're
using Bitcoin, doesn't make you immune from

00:51:52.680 --> 00:51:56.990
regulation, prosecution.

00:51:56.990 --> 00:52:03.359
I think, to me, that's like, "Duh."

00:52:03.359 --> 00:52:09.400
But, you know, I think there was kind of some
feeling that it would be this kind of magical

00:52:09.400 --> 00:52:14.780
fairy dust that would let you sidestep all
of the existing legal and regulatory framework.

00:52:14.780 --> 00:52:16.170
And that's just not true.

00:52:16.170 --> 00:52:17.730
REDIKER: How about tax?

00:52:17.730 --> 00:52:18.730
ANDRESEN: Tax?

00:52:18.730 --> 00:52:22.380
REDIKER: I mean, you know, regulation, know
your client, that's one thing.

00:52:22.380 --> 00:52:28.630
But, you know, societies generally exist with
at lest some element of tax revenue.

00:52:28.630 --> 00:52:36.080
Is there a general understanding and acceptance
of Bitcoin Foundation and the broad bitcoin

00:52:36.080 --> 00:52:41.680
universe, that even if it is an anonymous
transfer, there is an obligation at the end

00:52:41.680 --> 00:52:44.530
of the day to pay your fair share of taxes?

00:52:44.530 --> 00:52:45.530
ANDRESEN: Yes.

00:52:45.530 --> 00:52:49.630
I mean, my salary every month, I get paid
in bitcoin.

00:52:49.630 --> 00:52:54.530
So I get bitcoin transferred to one of my
Bitcoin addresses.

00:52:54.530 --> 00:53:00.150
But before that happens, your taxes are taken
out, and are paid in dollars, just as if I

00:53:00.150 --> 00:53:02.830
had any other job.

00:53:02.830 --> 00:53:13.000
And while we wish the IRS would be a little
more clear about exactly, you know, when taxable

00:53:13.000 --> 00:53:17.900
events occur, and I think that will happen.

00:53:17.900 --> 00:53:18.900
But yes.

00:53:18.900 --> 00:53:24.780
I mean, Bitcoin is really not all that different
from other things in the world that are taxed,

00:53:24.780 --> 00:53:30.400
like foreign currency transactions, or barter
transactions, or -- usually there's some kind

00:53:30.400 --> 00:53:35.210
of pigeon hole in the regulatory framework
that Bitcoin might not fit in perfectly, but

00:53:35.210 --> 00:53:37.000
probably fits in well enough.

00:53:37.000 --> 00:53:41.430
And taxing authorities all over the world
are currently scratching their heads and trying

00:53:41.430 --> 00:53:44.000
to figure out which pigeon hole to put Bitcoin
in.

00:53:44.000 --> 00:53:46.540
REDIKER: Gentleman in the back.

00:53:46.540 --> 00:53:49.590
QUESTION: Hey, Gavin.

00:53:49.590 --> 00:53:52.670
I'm John Flam (ph) with the Air Force.

00:53:52.670 --> 00:53:56.590
So you had mentioned earlier the term, "unsavory
people."

00:53:56.590 --> 00:54:00.859
And this -- we were talking about non-profits,
and how transparent these transactions are,

00:54:00.859 --> 00:54:02.200
which is great and all.

00:54:02.200 --> 00:54:06.650
But with that term, "unsavory people," one
thing that comes to mind, in my small brain

00:54:06.650 --> 00:54:11.970
anyways, is terrorist financing, money laundering,
those sorts of things.

00:54:11.970 --> 00:54:15.970
And so on the court of public opinion, it's
probably important that people want to contribute

00:54:15.970 --> 00:54:19.170
to an organization that, you know, does not
condone that, right.

00:54:19.170 --> 00:54:23.440
So with that said, like how -- and I'm sure
you've been asked this question before.

00:54:23.440 --> 00:54:30.349
How do you deter unsavory people from participating
in Bitcoin, and this type of transaction?

00:54:30.349 --> 00:54:37.140
ANDRESEN: That's a really good question for
law enforcement, and in the Senate hearings,

00:54:37.140 --> 00:54:41.710
end of last year, we heard from law enforcement
that they felt like they already have the

00:54:41.710 --> 00:54:46.570
tools that they need to deter people.

00:54:46.570 --> 00:54:51.540
And I think we've seen that with like the
closure of the Silk Road drug marketplace,

00:54:51.540 --> 00:54:59.960
where -- thinking about it purely from a technical
point of view, I think once you get to a size

00:54:59.960 --> 00:55:08.990
that's kind of large enough to be interesting,
it gets really hard to pull off whatever criminal

00:55:08.990 --> 00:55:12.580
enterprise, you know, you're trying to pull
off.

00:55:12.580 --> 00:55:18.650
And so I think we'll see naturally kind of
these little Silk Roads start out as this

00:55:18.650 --> 00:55:21.500
tiny little place on the hidden Internet.

00:55:21.500 --> 00:55:27.010
They got big enough that the founder had to
start hiring people to help with that customer

00:55:27.010 --> 00:55:29.590
support.

00:55:29.590 --> 00:55:33.839
Once you have more than like one or two people
involved, I think it becomes really hard for

00:55:33.839 --> 00:55:43.160
criminal organization to function, you know,
to not get caught; especially with a system

00:55:43.160 --> 00:55:49.150
like Bitcoin, where all these transactions
are being recorded in this global, public

00:55:49.150 --> 00:55:50.150
ledger.

00:55:50.150 --> 00:55:54.240
And the more -- just the more people you interact
with, the more opportunity there is for somebody

00:55:54.240 --> 00:55:59.790
to actually turn out to be working for law
enforcement, which I think is a big part of

00:55:59.790 --> 00:56:02.070
why Silk Road got taken down.

00:56:02.070 --> 00:56:09.540
So I think the standard law enforcement techniques
of infiltration, of surveillance, of all the

00:56:09.540 --> 00:56:16.630
other tools they have at their disposal, will
work with Bitcoin, just like they work with

00:56:16.630 --> 00:56:26.060
people who are smuggling diamonds, or transferring
cash across borders, or, you know, doing what

00:56:26.060 --> 00:56:28.420
other unsavory stuff.

00:56:28.420 --> 00:56:30.710
REDIKER: We've got time for one more question.

00:56:30.710 --> 00:56:34.810
Before I take it, I just want remind everyone
this meeting is on the record.

00:56:34.810 --> 00:56:36.450
So last question.

00:56:36.450 --> 00:56:38.860
Gentleman back here.

00:56:38.860 --> 00:56:40.470
QUESTION: Hi.

00:56:40.470 --> 00:56:41.589
I'm Garrett Graff.

00:56:41.589 --> 00:56:44.490
I'm editor of Washingtonian Magazine, and
a proud bitcoin owner.

00:56:44.490 --> 00:56:46.180
But I'm not Satoshi Nakamoto.

00:56:46.180 --> 00:56:49.920
So I can help you narrow that down a little
bit.

00:56:49.920 --> 00:56:50.920
I'm curious.

00:56:50.920 --> 00:56:53.840
We've talked about it in a couple of different
contexts.

00:56:53.840 --> 00:56:58.670
If you would just talk a little bit about,
as the -- while I understand, there are not

00:56:58.670 --> 00:57:01.310
"you guys" at Bitcoin.

00:57:01.310 --> 00:57:05.390
You are, to a certain extent, the public face
of sort of Bitcoin, Inc.

00:57:05.390 --> 00:57:09.950
And I'm curious if you would talk a little
bit about -- I assume you have had some level

00:57:09.950 --> 00:57:16.270
of interaction with various entities of the
United States government, and sort of what

00:57:16.270 --> 00:57:20.869
those interactions have been, and what the
reaction you've gotten from -- your interactions

00:57:20.869 --> 00:57:21.869
have been?

00:57:21.869 --> 00:57:26.450
ANDRESEN: Well, I gave a talk at the CIA a
couple years ago.

00:57:26.450 --> 00:57:32.830
And they're really good at not talking back.

00:57:32.830 --> 00:57:34.099
They're well trained.

00:57:34.099 --> 00:57:38.730
So, you know, on some level, I don't know.

00:57:38.730 --> 00:57:44.130
My interactions with regulators have typically
been very open.

00:57:44.130 --> 00:57:47.730
I mean, the fantastic thing about working
on Bitcoin is we really don't have any secrets,

00:57:47.730 --> 00:57:48.730
right.

00:57:48.730 --> 00:57:52.680
I mean, we're very upfront about -- we're
an open-source software project.

00:57:52.680 --> 00:57:53.680
Here's the code.

00:57:53.680 --> 00:57:55.109
Here's how it works.

00:57:55.109 --> 00:57:56.420
Here's where the money comes from.

00:57:56.420 --> 00:57:59.240
Here's where it goes.

00:57:59.240 --> 00:58:08.180
So it's pretty -- it's actually pretty easy
to talk to government, to just kind of put

00:58:08.180 --> 00:58:12.420
it out there as, you know, here's what the
technology does.

00:58:12.420 --> 00:58:18.640
We're not going to say what it means, or what's
going to happen in the future, but, you know,

00:58:18.640 --> 00:58:21.520
I think the interactions have been generally
pretty positive.

00:58:21.520 --> 00:58:24.770
I think they've generally been pretty positive
because we have been so open.

00:58:24.770 --> 00:58:29.890
I mean, we're not trying to do anything nefarious
here.

00:58:29.890 --> 00:58:36.320
And I think everybody working on -- at least
everybody I know working on Bitcoin is doing

00:58:36.320 --> 00:58:39.810
it for the reason that we think it'll make
the world a better place.

00:58:39.810 --> 00:58:46.130
We think it'll just make our lives better.

00:58:46.130 --> 00:58:50.700
And so, you know, whether we can convince
every regulator in the world of that or not,

00:58:50.700 --> 00:58:52.600
I don't think we will.

00:58:52.600 --> 00:58:59.710
But, you know, so far, at least here in the
U.S., it feels like, you know, we've been

00:58:59.710 --> 00:59:05.980
successful in that message, that we really
do think that the benefits of Bitcoin are

00:59:05.980 --> 00:59:07.450
going to make the world a better place.

00:59:07.450 --> 00:59:09.550
REDIKER: Fabulous way to end.

00:59:09.550 --> 00:59:14.349
I'm still a little bit of a skeptic on the
whether central banks play a positive role

00:59:14.349 --> 00:59:16.960
or not, but we'll leave that for another day.

00:59:16.960 --> 00:59:19.349
But I think we all learned a lot this morning.

00:59:19.349 --> 00:59:22.640
I think you put forth a great case.

00:59:22.640 --> 00:59:24.410
And we all wish you the best.

00:59:24.410 --> 00:59:25.410
And thank you all for coming.

00:59:25.410 --> 00:59:25.411
(APPLAUSE)

